::CW_U03_L02_Q01::Passage A: Tessa shared her lunch when she had packed only half a sandwich. Passage B: Andre gave his gloves to a neighbor waiting in the cold. What shared theme is best supported?{
~A. Food and gloves are identical.#Incorrect. The objects differ.
=B. Generosity can require giving up personal comfort.#Correct. Both characters sacrifice comfort to help someone else.
~C. Cold weather appears in both passages.#Incorrect. Only Passage B has cold weather.
~D. Lunch is always a theme.#Incorrect. Lunch is a detail.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q02::Passage A: Mira confessed that she copied the drawing. Passage B: Jonah told the coach he had stepped out of bounds. How are the themes similar?{
~A. Both are about art only.#Incorrect. Only Passage A involves drawing.
~B. Both prove rules do not matter.#Incorrect. The characters respect truth and rules.
~C. Both are about winning prizes.#Incorrect. Prizes are not mentioned.
=D. Both show honesty when silence would be easier.#Correct. Both characters admit wrongdoing.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q03::Passage A: A girl plants seeds in a vacant lot. Passage B: A boy repairs one bench in an abandoned park. What shared theme is strongest?{
=A. Renewal can begin with one small act.#Correct. Both characters improve neglected places through small actions.
~B. Parks and lots are the same word.#Incorrect. The places differ.
~C. Seeds repair benches.#Incorrect. This mixes details inaccurately.
~D. Abandoned places cannot change.#Incorrect. Both passages show change beginning.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q04::Passage A: Nina keeps practicing after failure. Passage B: Leo asks for advice after failure. Which comparison is strongest?{
~A. Both passages are the same because both names have four letters.#Incorrect. Names are surface details.
~B. Passage B has no theme because Leo asks for help.#Incorrect. Asking for help can develop a theme.
=C. Both show growth after failure, but Nina relies on persistence while Leo relies on support.#Correct. This names the shared theme and different development.
~D. Failure always ends effort.#Incorrect. Both characters continue.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q05::Passage A: The queen removes her crown to listen to the farmers. Passage B: The principal sits in a student desk before changing the school rule. What shared theme is best supported?{
=A. Leadership requires listening beyond one's own position.#Correct. Both leaders lower distance and listen.
~B. Crowns and desks are the same object.#Incorrect. They are different symbols.
~C. Farmers always attend school.#Incorrect. This combines unrelated details.
~D. Leaders should never change.#Incorrect. Both passages suggest change through listening.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q06::Passage A: Omar keeps his grandfather's watch and finally starts it again. Passage B: Lila cooks from her aunt's recipe in a new city. What theme do both passages develop?{
~A. Watches and recipes are worthless.#Incorrect. Both objects matter.
~B. New cities erase memory.#Incorrect. Lila carries memory into a new place.
~C. Time and food cannot appear in stories.#Incorrect. They are meaningful details.
=D. Memory can help people carry loved ones forward.#Correct. The watch and recipe connect memory to present action.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q07::Passage A: Ben apologizes to the friend he ignored. Passage B: Cara returns the letter she hid from her sister. What shared idea is strongest?{
~A. Letters and friends are identical.#Incorrect. They are different details.
=B. Repair begins when people take responsibility for harm.#Correct. Both characters correct a harmful choice.
~C. Apologies are never useful.#Incorrect. The passages move toward repair.
~D. Sisters cannot be in themes.#Incorrect. Relationships can develop themes.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q08::Passage A: A runner slows down to help a fallen competitor. Passage B: A singer pauses her solo so another performer can recover the forgotten line. What comparison is best?{
~A. Running and singing are the same activity.#Incorrect. The activities differ.
~B. Only Passage A has a character.#Incorrect. Both have characters.
=C. Both passages show compassion interrupting personal success.#Correct. Each character risks achievement to help someone else.
~D. Forgotten lines are sports equipment.#Incorrect. This misreads details.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q09::Passage A: The narrator leaves a familiar town but keeps the old house key. Passage B: The narrator moves to a new country but plants seeds from home. Which shared theme is strongest?{
~A. Keys grow into plants.#Incorrect. This inaccurately merges details.
~B. Moving means forgetting everything.#Incorrect. Both narrators keep memory.
~C. Houses and countries are identical.#Incorrect. The scale differs.
=D. Change can include carrying part of the past forward.#Correct. The key and seeds preserve connection.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q10::Passage A: A student admits she needs a tutor. Passage B: A musician asks the orchestra to start again after his mistake. What shared theme is best supported?{
=A. Growth often begins with humility.#Correct. Both characters admit need or error.
~B. Tutors always play music.#Incorrect. This mixes details.
~C. Mistakes should be hidden.#Incorrect. Both characters acknowledge difficulty.
~D. Orchestras only appear in schools.#Incorrect. The passage does not say that.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q11::Passage A: A father sells his motorcycle to pay for his daughter's books. Passage B: A sister misses a concert to care for her brother. What shared theme is strongest?{
~A. Concerts and motorcycles are the same.#Incorrect. The details differ.
~B. Books cause every sacrifice.#Incorrect. Books appear only in Passage A.
=C. Love can require sacrifice.#Correct. Both characters give up something personally valuable.
~D. Family love has no theme.#Incorrect. Family choices often develop theme.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q12::Passage A: After being excluded, Mina invites another lonely student to sit with her. Passage B: After moving often, Theo welcomes every new neighbor first. What comparison is strongest?{
~A. Both passages are only about chairs.#Incorrect. Seating is not the main theme.
=B. Both characters use their own loneliness to create belonging for others.#Correct. Their experiences shape compassionate action.
~C. Moving and lunch are identical.#Incorrect. The experiences differ.
~D. New neighbors never need welcome.#Incorrect. Passage B shows welcome matters.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q13::Passage A: A guard opens the gate after recognizing the refugee's song. Passage B: A teacher changes the lesson after hearing a student's story. What shared theme fits?{
=A. Listening to another person's story can change what people choose to do.#Correct. Both passages show response after hearing another voice.
~B. Gates and lessons are the same object.#Incorrect. The details differ.
~C. Songs always happen in classrooms.#Incorrect. Only Passage A has a song.
~D. Teachers never change lessons.#Incorrect. Passage B says the teacher changes it.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q14::Passage A: A child returns a stolen coin. Passage B: A merchant corrects a customer's overpayment. Which comparison is strongest?{
~A. Coins and customers are the same.#Incorrect. They are different details.
~B. Only children can be honest.#Incorrect. The merchant is honest too.
~C. Money removes theme.#Incorrect. Money can create an integrity conflict.
=D. Both passages show integrity through returning what does not belong to the character.#Correct. Both choices restore fairness.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q15::Passage A: A girl refuses to laugh at a cruel joke. Passage B: A boy tells his friends to stop mocking a classmate. What shared theme is strongest?{
~A. Jokes are always kind.#Incorrect. The joke is cruel.
~B. Friends never influence choices.#Incorrect. The group creates pressure.
=C. Courage can mean resisting a group that is doing harm.#Correct. Both characters oppose cruelty.
~D. Classmates cannot be in fiction.#Incorrect. School situations can develop themes.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q16::Passage A: The town rebuilds a bridge together. Passage B: The neighbors cook meals for a family after a fire. What shared theme is best supported?{
~A. Bridges are meals.#Incorrect. They are different images.
=B. Communities grow stronger when people share the work of recovery.#Correct. Both passages show collective help after hardship.
~C. Fires build bridges.#Incorrect. This misreads the details.
~D. Recovery is always private.#Incorrect. Both passages show community recovery.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q17::Passage A: A student rewrites the essay after harsh feedback. Passage B: A dancer practices the step after public criticism. What shared theme fits?{
~A. Essays and dances are identical.#Incorrect. They are different forms.
~B. Feedback should always be ignored.#Incorrect. Both characters use feedback.
~C. Public moments cannot teach anything.#Incorrect. Passage B shows learning after a public moment.
=D. Criticism can become useful when a person uses it to improve.#Correct. Both characters respond by working.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q18::Passage A: A traveler gives away the map after memorizing the road. Passage B: A cook teaches the recipe before leaving town. Which shared theme is strongest?{
=A. Knowledge becomes more valuable when it is shared.#Correct. Both characters pass useful knowledge to others.
~B. Maps are recipes.#Incorrect. They are different forms of knowledge.
~C. Leaving means taking everything with you.#Incorrect. Both characters share before leaving.
~D. Roads are food.#Incorrect. This combines details incorrectly.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q19::Passage A: A prince admits fear before entering battle. Passage B: A firefighter says she is afraid before opening the door. How are the themes similar?{
~A. Princes and firefighters have the same job.#Incorrect. Their roles differ.
=B. Both show courage as action taken while fear is present.#Correct. Neither character is fearless, but both act.
~C. Fear prevents all action.#Incorrect. Both characters move forward.
~D. Doors and battles are the same setting.#Incorrect. The settings differ.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q20::Passage A: A narrator keeps the cracked bowl from her childhood kitchen. Passage B: A narrator saves the song her mother used to hum. What shared theme fits?{
~A. Bowls are songs.#Incorrect. The details differ.
~B. Childhood has no meaning.#Incorrect. The passages show memory matters.
=C. Ordinary details can hold powerful memories.#Correct. The bowl and song connect to memory.
~D. Songs cannot be remembered.#Incorrect. Passage B centers on a remembered song.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q21::Passage A: The hero refuses the throne to remain with the village. Passage B: The captain declines promotion to protect her crew. What comparison is strongest?{
=A. Both passages show leadership as service rather than status.#Correct. Both characters reject higher status to serve others.
~B. Thrones and ships are identical.#Incorrect. The settings differ.
~C. Promotions always mean danger.#Incorrect. The passage focuses on choice and service.
~D. Villages cannot have leaders.#Incorrect. The hero serves the village.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q22::Passage A: A sister waits outside the audition room holding a lucky ribbon. Passage B: A brother records every practice so his sibling can improve. What shared theme is best supported?{
~A. Ribbons and recordings are the same.#Incorrect. They are different support details.
~B. Auditions never need practice.#Incorrect. Passage B includes practice.
=C. Support can appear through quiet actions, not only advice.#Correct. Both siblings help without taking over.
~D. Siblings always compete.#Incorrect. These siblings support.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q23::Passage A: A farmer saves seeds after a failed harvest. Passage B: A student keeps notes after failing a test. What shared theme fits?{
~A. Seeds are tests.#Incorrect. They are different details.
~B. Failure ends every future.#Incorrect. Both passages prepare for another chance.
~C. Notes only matter on farms.#Incorrect. Notes matter in Passage B.
=D. Hope can survive failure when people prepare for another chance.#Correct. Seeds and notes both prepare for future effort.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q24::Passage A: A character burns an old invitation after finally saying goodbye. Passage B: A character packs a photo carefully before moving away. Which comparison is strongest?{
~A. Burning and packing mean exactly the same action.#Incorrect. The actions differ.
=B. Both passages explore letting go, but one uses release while the other uses carrying memory forward.#Correct. This compares theme development through different actions.
~C. Invitations and photos cannot carry meaning.#Incorrect. Both objects are meaningful.
~D. Moving always means forgetting.#Incorrect. Passage B preserves memory.
}
::CW_U03_L02_Q25::Passage A: A student speaks up when the group excludes a classmate. Passage B: A neighbor opens her door when everyone else looks away. What shared theme is strongest?{
=A. Belonging often depends on someone choosing inclusion over silence.#Correct. Both characters act when others exclude or ignore someone.
~B. Doors and classmates are identical.#Incorrect. These are different details.
~C. Silence always helps people belong.#Incorrect. The passages value action over silence.
~D. Neighbors cannot appear in themes.#Incorrect. Neighbor actions can develop universal themes.
}
